# Built from the publisher's revised template example.
BOOK = '1 Timothy'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'letters'
SUBTITLE = 'Sound Doctrine, Order in the Church, and the Good Fight'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', '1 Timothy is a letter from <b>Paul</b>, “an apostle of Jesus Christ by the commandment of God our Saviour, and Lord Jesus Christ” (1 Tim. 1:1), written to <b>Timothy</b>, “my own son in the faith” (1 Tim. 1:2). Paul had left Timothy in <b>Ephesus</b> to charge certain men “that they teach no other doctrine” (1 Tim. 1:3). The letter addresses sound doctrine, prayer, church order, qualifications for overseers and deacons, godliness, and the good fight of faith.'), ('p', '1 Timothy belongs to the <b>Body of Christ (Pauline) program</b>, based on its audience markers and Pauline authorship (cf. 1 Tim. 1:1–2; 2 Tim. 1:11).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of 1 Timothy', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the letter — how Paul begins, how his instructions develop, and how the letter builds toward its final charge. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Sound Doctrine and the Law (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'Paul charges Timothy to confront false doctrine (1 Tim. 1:3–4), explains that “the law is good, if a man use it lawfully” (1 Tim. 1:8), and gives his testimony of mercy (1 Tim. 1:12–16).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Prayer and Order in the Church (Chs. 2–3)'), ('p', 'Paul urges prayer “for all men” and “for kings” (1 Tim. 2:1–2), teaches that “there is one God, and one mediator” (1 Tim. 2:5), and sets qualifications for bishops and deacons (1 Tim. 3:1–13).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Mystery of Godliness and False Teaching (3:14–4:16)'), ('p', 'Paul describes the church as “the pillar and ground of the truth” (1 Tim. 3:15), declares the “mystery of godliness” (1 Tim. 3:16), warns of departures from the faith (1 Tim. 4:1), and charges Timothy to take heed to doctrine (1 Tim. 4:16).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Care, Contentment, and the Good Fight (Chs. 5–6)'), ('p', 'Paul gives instructions for widows (1 Tim. 5:3–16), elders (1 Tim. 5:17–20), servants (1 Tim. 6:1–2), warns about the love of money (1 Tim. 6:10), and commands Timothy to “fight the good fight of faith” (1 Tim. 6:12).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in 1 Timothy', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas Paul emphasizes in this book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how the Body of Christ should think and live. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas Paul wants Timothy (and us) to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Sound Doctrine</b> — “Teach no other doctrine” (1 Tim. 1:3).', '<b>Prayer for All</b> — Supplications and prayers “for all men” (1 Tim. 2:1–2).', '<b>The House of God</b> — The church is “the pillar and ground of the truth” (1 Tim. 3:15).', '<b>Godliness</b> — “Godliness with contentment is great gain” (1 Tim. 6:6).', '<b>The Good Fight</b> — “Fight the good fight of faith” (1 Tim. 6:12).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in 1 Timothy', [('p', 'This section gathers what the letter teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how Paul presents Him to the church. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how 1 Timothy lifts up Christ as Saviour, Mediator, Ransom, and the One revealed in the mystery of godliness.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Saviour</b> — “Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners” (1 Tim. 1:15).', '<b>The One Mediator</b> — “One mediator between God and men” (1 Tim. 2:5).', '<b>A Ransom for All</b> — He “gave himself a ransom for all” (1 Tim. 2:6).', '<b>God Manifest in the Flesh</b> — “God was manifest in the flesh” (1 Tim. 3:16).', '<b>The Saviour of All Men</b> — “The Saviour of all men, specially of those that believe” (1 Tim. 4:10).'])]),
(5, 'The Church, the Law, and Israel in 1 Timothy', [('p', 'This section helps you see how 1 Timothy speaks about the church, the law, and Israel without mixing their programs. Paul mentions the law and gives instructions for the church, but he never places believers under Israel’s covenants or prophetic promises. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read 1 Timothy in its own setting — as a Body-of-Christ letter — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about Israel’s program.'), ('ul', ['The law is good “if a man use it lawfully” (1 Tim. 1:8).', 'The church is “the pillar and ground of the truth” (1 Tim. 3:15).', 'Paul is a pattern for those who believe (1 Tim. 1:16).', 'The letter does <b>not</b> place the church under Israel’s covenants (cf. 1 Tim. 2:5–6).', 'The audience is the Body of Christ, not Israel under the law.'])]),
(6, 'Body-of-Christ Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in 1 Timothy that belong uniquely to the Body of Christ — truths Paul gives to the church in this present age. These points show what is distinctively Pauline in the letter: one mediator, prayer for all, qualifications for church leaders, the mystery of godliness, and the good fight of faith. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how 1 Timothy contributes to the doctrine and order of the Body of Christ without borrowing from Israel’s prophetic program.'), ('ul', ['One God and one mediator (1 Tim. 2:5).', 'Prayer for all men (1 Tim. 2:1–2).', 'Qualifications for bishops and deacons (1 Tim. 3:1–13).', 'The mystery of godliness (1 Tim. 3:16).', 'Godliness with contentment (1 Tim. 6:6).', 'The good fight of faith (1 Tim. 6:12).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What 1 Timothy Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in 1 Timothy and shows which ones belong to the Body of Christ. Each row lists a truth from the letter, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Paul’s audience and setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what 1 Timothy teaches directly to the church today and keeps those truths separate from universal principles or reports that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in 1 Timothy', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Christ Came to Save', 'Christ Jesus came to save sinners', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '1 Tim. 1:15'], ['One Mediator', 'One mediator between God and men', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '1 Tim. 2:5'], ['The Church', 'The church is the pillar and ground of truth', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '1 Tim. 3:15'], ['Mystery of Godliness', 'God was manifest in the flesh', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '1 Tim. 3:16'], ['Departing from the Faith', 'Some shall depart from the faith', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '1 Tim. 4:1'], ['Qualifications', 'Bishop must be blameless', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '1 Tim. 3:1–7'], ['The Law', 'The law is good if used lawfully', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '1 Tim. 1:8'], ['The Good Fight', 'Fight the good fight of faith', 'Body of Christ (Pauline) Truth', '1 Tim. 6:12']], [20, 38, 26, 16])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in 1 Timothy that apply to everyone, not just the Body of Christ. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Paul’s program-specific instructions, this section helps you see what 1 Timothy teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches the church in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Pray for All', 'Pray for all men', '1 Tim. 2:1'], ['Godliness with Contentment', 'Godliness with contentment is great gain', '1 Tim. 6:6'], ['Love of Money', 'The love of money is the root of all evil', '1 Tim. 6:10'], ['Provide for Family', 'Provide for your own household', '1 Tim. 5:8'], ['Be an Example', 'Be an example in word, conduct, love, faith, purity', '1 Tim. 4:12'], ['Take Heed', 'Take heed to yourself and the doctrine', '1 Tim. 4:16'], ['Honor Those Who Labor', 'Elders who rule well deserve honor', '1 Tim. 5:17'], ['Good Works', 'Be rich in good works', '1 Tim. 6:18']], [26, 50, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of 1 Timothy without mixing programs. The applications come directly from Paul’s instructions to the Body of Christ and from the universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the letter actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants or prophetic program — this grid helps you respond to 1 Timothy in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Hold Sound Doctrine</b> — Keep to the doctrine taught (1 Tim. 1:3; 4:16).', '<b>Pray for All</b> — Pray for all men and those in authority (1 Tim. 2:1–2).', '<b>Order the Church Well</b> — Follow the qualifications for leaders (1 Tim. 3:1–13).', '<b>Honor Those Who Labor</b> — Honor elders who rule well (1 Tim. 5:17).', '<b>Care for Family</b> — Provide for your own household (1 Tim. 5:8).', '<b>Be an Example</b> — Live as an example in word, conduct, love, faith, purity (1 Tim. 4:12).', '<b>Pursue Godliness with Contentment</b> — Resist the love of money (1 Tim. 6:6–10).', '<b>Fight the Good Fight</b> — Lay hold on eternal life (1 Tim. 6:12).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for 1 Timothy', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach 1 Timothy in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['A Charge Against Other Doctrine (1 Tim. 1:1–20)', 'Prayer for All (1 Tim. 2:1–8)', 'Bishops and Deacons (1 Tim. 3:1–13)', 'The House of God (1 Tim. 3:14–16)', 'Departing and Holding On (1 Tim. 4:1–16)', 'Widows, Elders, and Servants (1 Tim. 5:1–6:2)', 'Contentment and the Good Fight (1 Tim. 6:3–21)'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read 1 Timothy, helping you see both what the letter says to believers and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications draw only on what 1 Timothy itself says, so your response stays tied to the text.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching 1 Timothy simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group see what the letter states and what it leaves unsaid, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap framework, 1 Timothy was written during the overlap from Paul to a younger minister. This Companion shows you how to read the letter’s charges and church order in their own setting while drawing out the principles that apply to all believers.')])]
